    <text>
        <body>
            <div xml:id="d628">

                <pb facs="0817" n="727" sameAs="frus:frus1934v03/pg727"/>

                <note rend="inline" type="source">893.6363 Manchuria/36: Telegram</note>
                <head><hi rendition="#italic">The Acting Secretary of State to the Ambassador in
                        Japan</hi> (<hi rendition="#italic">Grew</hi>)</head>
                <opener>
                    <dateline rendition="#float-right"><placeName><hi rendition="#small-caps"
                                >Washington</hi></placeName>, <date calendar="gregorian"
                            when="1934-08-29T17:00:00-04:00">August 29, 1934—5
                        p.m.</date></dateline>
                </opener>
                <p>149. Your 185, August 23, 10 a.m.,<anchor n="24" xml:id="d628-f1"/> and previous,
                    especially 168, August 3, 6 p.m.</p>
                <p>While giving careful consideration to the thoughtful suggestions made in your
                    182, August 20, 3 p.m., the Department inclines to the view that the American
                    Government should not omit reply to the informal memorandum of the Japanese
                    Foreign Office of August 2.<anchor n="25" xml:id="d628-f2"/> The Department has
                    examined with approval the British Government’s reply, and it seems to us that
                    this Government should also reply, for the purposes, <hi rendition="#italic"
                        >inter alia</hi>, of keeping the record clear as between ourselves and the
                    Japanese and demonstrating willingness to cooperate as between ourselves and the
                    British. Also, the placing on record at this time of a reply might be useful in
                    connection with the question of possible implementing of the suggestions made in
                    your 182, August 20, 3 p.m.</p>
                <p>Unless you perceive substantial reason for further discussion of this question
                    and/or of the text submitted below, in which case inform the Department promptly
                    of your views, please give or send promptly, in such manner as you may consider
                    appropriate, to the Minister for Foreign Affairs, an informal memorandum as
                    follows:</p>
                <p>[Here follows text of memorandum dated August 31, 1934, printed in <ref
                        target="frus:frus1931-41v01/pg_133"><hi rendition="#italic">Foreign
                            Relations</hi>, Japan, 1931–1941, volume I, page 133</ref>.]</p>
                <p>Inform Department and Peiping of action taken.</p>
                <closer><signed><hi rendition="#small-caps">Phillips</hi></signed></closer>
            </div>
        </body>
    </text>
